            <div xml:id="d302">

                <pb facs="0412" n="402" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg402"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.003/1037: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-12-15T15:00:00+01:00">December 15,
                            1942—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 15—12:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7090. Mr. Eden wrote me on December 14 with respect to the penultimate paragraph
                    of the memorandum transmitted in my 6608, November 24, 2 p.m., mentioning that
                    the Chinese Government had <pb facs="0413" n="403" xml:id="pg403"/> asked for
                    the inclusion in article 5 of the Sino-British treaty an additional clause
                    terminating the convention for the extension of Hong Kong territory signed at
                    Peiping in 1898. Mr. Eden now states that the British Government is not prepared
                    to consider this matter in connection with the present treaty, since not only
                    has the area leased by the convention nothing to do with extraterritoriality,
                    but being, as stated in the agreement, an enlargement of British territory, is
                    in an entirely different category from the concessions and settlements in China
                    in which the British are relinquishing their special rights. The British
                    Ambassador at Chungking has therefore been instructed to inform the Chinese
                    Government that the British Government regards the leased territory as outside
                    the scope of the present treaty.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
